# Local adaptation (this release's lldb overlay predates the `includes`
# layout, UtilityHeaders, and the Fortran/Telemetry/ValueObject targets) of:
# Upstream-Commit: https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/commit/9c87726e2532fbd9cc1d94e09c8da575c8e5c0d2
# Upstream-Commit: https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/commit/ba4f8cc96397c430aad36f6402559e08e6fd0706
#
# "[lldb] [bazel] Remove cycles in Bazel by merging targets" (#229101) and
# "[lldb][bazel] Merge cyclic core libraries into one target" (#237392), plus
# the stale `Interpreter -> API` edge removal from #228580 (7bbbca018d8e)
# that the merge needs. lldb's Breakpoint/Commands/Core/DataFormatters/
# Expression/Interpreter/Symbol/Target libraries and the clang plugins only
# build as separate cc_library targets because header-only `*Headers` shims
# hide their cycles from Bazel. lld resolves the resulting backward
# references; `ld.gold`, which Bazel's GCC toolchain picks on the Linux
# images, does not, so liblldb fails to link with hundreds of undefined
# `lldb_private::CommandInterpreter`/`OptionValue*` symbols while CMake
# (which repeats libraries, LINK_INTERFACE_MULTIPLICITY on lldbCore) links
# fine. Upstream merged the core libraries into `CoreLibraries` and the
# clang plugins into `PluginsClang`, keeping the old names as aliases; the
# `UnixSignals` subclasses move into `CoreLibraries` as upstream did.
# Differences from upstream: `CoreLibraries` uses `includes` because its
# headers come from both `include/` and `source/`; the plugin header shims
# that `source/Core` and `source/Expression` still include at this release
# (CPlusPlusLanguage.h, ObjCLanguage.h, DWARFUnit.h) stay, with
# `PluginSymbolFileDWARFHeaders` pointed at `CoreHeaders`. Those same
# includes are why `lldb-server`, which has no force-linked API objects to
# pull the plugins in first, still fails under gold at this release.
